\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
This paper examines the extent to which local large language models (LLMs) can be used to guide sensitive decisions, such as exercise recommendation and the design of fitness and nutrition regimes following injury. Privacy, hallucination, depth of response and readability are inherent LLM concerns, and each is examined throughout. The investigation is conducted through an AI-first health application, ReFit, built on local LLMs. The application employs techniques including retrieval augmented generation (RAG), fine-tuning, and system prompts that encourage explainability in order to mitigate these issues. ReFit's outputs are assessed to determine which technique exerts the greatest effect on mitigating each underlying LLM issue.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Large Language sadasdasasd Model, AI, Local LLM, Ollama, Llama, Qwen, Safety, Privacy, Hallucination, RAG, fine-tuning
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Artificial intelligence (AI), in the form of large language models (LLMs), has become difficult to avoid in everyday online activity over the past three years. On retail platforms, such as Amazon, user reviews are summarised before the customer reads them; on Google, a summary of the front page of results is generated automatically, in some cases producing errors that are both conspicuous and potentially dangerous \citep{hart}. An underlying risk of misleading or erroneous output is inherent to LLMs and arises from their architecture---they predict the next token in a sequence probabilistically \citep{bender2021}. Such responses are termed hallucinations \citep{ji2023} and are associated with a decline in user trust. Survey evidence indicates that users are broadly willing to accept AI assistance in decision making, yet remain markedly sceptical of delegating decisions to an AI system outright \citep{aci}. The objective of this report is to examine the methods deployed to minimise dangerous hallucinations, and to measure the extent to which an AI-first application specialising in injury accommodation, workout planning and nutrition can provide safe advice.

\section{Research}
\label{sec:research}
The research undertaken for this report may be divided into four categories: local and open LLMs, reliability and privacy concerns, agentic principles and fine-tuning, and existing applications of AI within sport and medicine.

\subsection{Local/Open LLMs}
\label{sec:localllm}
\citet{touvron2023llama} describe how ``for a given compute budget, the best performances are not achieved by the largest models, but by smaller models trained on more data'' \citep[p.~1]{touvron2023llama}. This principle, together with the model being exclusively trained on publicly available data, established Llama as the first open LLM that is capable of running locally on-device while remaining competitive with proprietary cloud-based models. Llama2 \citep{touvron2023llama2} extends this work by incorporating instruction-tuned dialogue, producing assistant-like conversational behaviour, and Llama3.1 \citep{grattafiori2024} is the model adopted in this project due to its further improved instruction-following capabilities.

BioMistral \citep{labrak2024} is the medical LLM selected for this project on the basis of its benchmark performance and conversational ability. Given the sensitive nature of the application's domain, a medically fine-tuned model serves as a safeguard in the event that general-purpose models fail to identify an issue.

Qwen2.5 \citep{qwen2025} is a further open-source model employed in this project. It is fine-tuned for instruction following and possesses a broad knowledge base, providing a counterpart to BioMistral and Llama3.1 in the review and error-detection stages.

Qwen2.5-VL \citep{bai2025} is the model employed for vision tasks in this project, as it is among the leading vision-specialist models currently available and integrates well with the corresponding text-only Qwen model.

\subsection{Reliability and Privacy}
\label{sec:reliability}
\citet{ji2023} define the two principal types of hallucination present in LLM outputs: intrinsic hallucination, in which the generated output contradicts the source content, and extrinsic hallucination, in which the generated output cannot be verified against the source content. Two further concepts are central to this work: faithfulness, denoting consistency with the provided source, and factuality, denoting the extent to which the output is grounded in fact. The application is evaluated against both. The paper emphasises that ungrounded LLMs should not be permitted to generate output without constraints, particularly in medical settings, as this may result in severe harm.

Ungrounded LLMs also present a security risk. Both \citet{carlini2021} and \citet{nasr2023} demonstrate that LLMs can be induced to disclose sensitive information such as telephone numbers and passwords with comparatively little effort, and that larger base models memorise a greater proportion of their training data than models with fewer parameters. Safeguards and system prompts are implemented in commercially available LLMs, such as ChatGPT \citep{nasr2023}, but the risk remains inherent to models trained on private data.

\subsection{Agentic Principles and Fine-tuning}
\label{sec:agentic}
\citet{lewis2021} present the foundational work on RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation), a form of language generation that grounds a model in a source of truth and requires it to consult that source before generating output for the end-user. This work popularised the concept, and RAG has since become one of the most widely deployed methods for reducing hallucination. It is not, however, sufficient in isolation, as retrieval may return incorrect data and the model may misinterpret the material retrieved \citep{ji2023}.

One method for improving factuality and reducing intrinsic hallucination is fine-tuning, in which all parameters of a pre-trained model are updated to suit a new task. This is, however, time- and resource-intensive \citep{hu2021}. \citet{hu2021} introduce Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), which, rather than updating all of the model's weights, freezes them and injects small trainable matrices alongside them, thereby greatly reducing the number of trainable parameters. This substantially reduces training time, although the full model must still be held in memory at 16-bit precision. \citet{dettmers2023} introduce the concept of QLoRA, which quantises a model to 4-bit precision, accelerating fine-tuning as LoRA does but with markedly lower memory consumption and with negligible loss. A comparison between a base and fine-tuned model is undertaken in this project.

\citet{yao2023} introduce ReAct, which comprises two steps. Having been given a task, the model first performs an explicit reasoning step in order to determine an optimal solution. It then calls a function to gather external information, before repeating the first step to observe and reason over the newly gathered information. This cycle continues until the model produces an answer. The authors report that the approach substantially increases output success rates relative to models employing only one of the two steps.

\subsection{AI in Medicine and Sports}
\label{sec:aimedicine}
\citet{liu2024medical} identify several benefits of employing medically fine-tuned LLMs as decision assistants rather than decision-makers, with particular attention to the potential for predicting disease and injury before onset through the analysis of large volumes of patient data. Substantive concerns are also raised, including the prevention of leakage of sensitive health information, integration of LLMs into existing systems, the ``black box'' nature of AI and the resulting interpretability and accountability concerns, and the risk posed by model bias. Reinforcement learning, where the model learns from clinicians' feedback, is proposed as one means of mitigation.

\citet{zaleski2024} test the comprehensiveness, accuracy and readability of a then widely used AI chatbot, with mixed results. The recommendations produced were generally accurate, but omitted important detail, such as exercise frequency, exercise type and target intensity. Readability of responses was reported at US college level, which is regarded as too advanced for most members of the general public likely to use such models. The most frequently reported source of misinformation in the study was the assertion that clinical approval must be acquired before undertaking any form of exercise. These results were obtained without any form of RAG or fine-tuning, underlining the need to ground LLMs in a base source of truth and to impose strict formatting boundaries.

A scoping review of 11 studies examining AI-assisted exercise recommendation \citep{lai2025} indicates that this remains an emerging field. The review concludes that, while AI may reduce clinician workload and extend provision to remote communities, the issues outlined in previous studies persist. It further reports that LLMs exhibit a bias toward young and healthy individuals, producing recommendations that are excessively strenuous for elderly or chronically ill users. This restricts the current scope of such systems largely to advisory tools, to be used with caution under clinical observation.

The research reviewed above informs an investigation into the extent to which an AI-first system can be used safely. To this end, a health application focused on users with prior injuries has been developed, under the name ReFit.

\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}
ReFit is designed to address the issues identified in the research above: the reduction of hallucination, the lack of depth in responses, the provision of reliable retrieval that the model does not disregard, the handling of sensitive data, and the avoidance of actively harmful advice.

\subsection{Requirements}
\label{sec:requirements}
The principal use case of the application is to provide users with tailored fitness advice and to generate exercise and nutrition plans that do not endanger user safety. The application must be grounded in a source of truth from which it does not deviate, and the user experience should be seamless and require minimal learning, with the majority of interactions taking place through a chat interface. Given that one of the principal benefits of AI and LLMs is the substitution of natural language for formal syntax, this affordance is deliberately tested to its limits in order to assess the ethical capabilities, or limitations, of LLMs as of 2026.

\subsection{Design}
\label{sec:design}
The landing page of the application consists of a chat window employing a warm colour palette, which supports media upload in the form of images or video. The user may then converse with the LLMs and receive advice tailored to their needs, with the system building a user profile over time and posing clarifying questions where appropriate.

The user may modify the knowledge stored in the application, including exercise difficulty ratings and the addition of new injuries or health conditions, through natural language, obviating the need to learn a formal syntax. The option to amend plans and details manually is retained should the user wish to use it, but the principal intended mode of interaction is a dialogue in which the application acts as an assistant that performs these operations on the user's behalf.

Users are able to view the exercises contained in their plan and to interact with them in order to rate their difficulty. Initial ratings are assigned on the basis of the number of muscles involved and whether the exercise is compound or isolation: a bicep curl, for example, is initially rated `easy' and a barbell squat `hard'. Accordingly, a user who experiences pain when performing a curl may reassign its difficulty to `medium' or `hard'.

Users are additionally able to view the macronutrient content of foods in a nutrition tab, allowing any AI output to be cross-checked against the database.

\subsection{Implementation}
\label{sec:implementation}
The frontend is implemented in React and TypeScript, using React hooks and TSX files rather than HTML files to manage page transitions.

The backend consists of Python functions that call local models through Ollama, which operate as agents with distinct roles. A principal concern in the use of LLMs in a medical capacity is the handling of sensitive user information \citep{liu2024medical}. ReFit addresses this by storing all user-related information in a database protected by an authentication encryption scheme: AES-128-CBC with HMAC-SHA256, via Fernet, and processing all data locally, on-device. Fernet was selected because it handles both confidentiality and data integrity automatically, thereby avoiding common and potentially devastating cryptographic design errors \citep{fernet}. Passwords are stored as hashes.

Information is only decrypted as required, and no user data is transmitted to an external server, substantially reducing the risk of an interception attack. The trade-off of this approach is that users require a relatively modern device, preferably with a GPU, and at least 20\,GB of free storage.

The models employed in ReFit are Llama3.1, BioMistral, Qwen2.5 and Qwen2.5-VL. They are not permitted to operate solely on their pretrained knowledge. RAG is used to ground them in a Postgres database, the contents of which are drawn from ExRx.net \citep{exrx}---an open-access repository of exercises indexed by target muscle, together with the UK government's nutrition database \citep{cofid} and its guidance on a balanced diet \citep{eatwell}. Each exercise entry records the exercise name, a description comprising step-by-step instructions and recommended form, the exercise type (compound or isolation), a difficulty rating that may be updated by the user, and the equipment required to perform it. Rating updates are implemented by inserting a row into a separate user-rating table which is queried subsequently, leaving the original table unmodified.

Each nutrition entry records the name, food group and description of the item, together with its calorie, protein, fat, carbohydrate, total sugar and fibre content.

All entries are encoded using Ollama's nomic-embed-text encoder \citep{nomic}, with the encoding stored as the final column of each entry. When the user sends a message, it is encoded, and the three closest matches are retrieved through an SQL query and stored in a retrievable variable. The full pipeline is summarised in Table~\ref{tab:pipeline}.

\begin{table*}[!t]
\caption{Stages of the ReFit pipeline, showing the model responsible for each stage and its inputs and outputs.}
\label{tab:pipeline}
\centering
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}l l X X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Stage} & \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Input} & \textbf{Output} \\
\midrule
Intent classification & Llama3.1 (base) & User query & Intent label (exercise, injury, nutrition, plan, plan-with-injury, general) \\
Retrieval & nomic-embed-text + Postgres & Encoded query & Three closest exercise or nutrition entries \\
First response & Llama3.1 (fine-tuned) & Query, condensed history, retrieved context & Initial advice under a strict system prompt \\
Review & Qwen2.5 & Initial advice, retrieved context & JSON: issues, verdict, corrected response \\
Rewrite & Llama3.1 (fine-tuned) & Verified advice & Streamed conversational markup \\
Plan generation & Llama3.1 (base, constrained decoding) & Completed intake schema, retrieved exercises & Plan JSON (exercise, day, sets, reps) \\
Image analysis & Qwen2.5-VL & Image and accompanying query & Form, physique or macronutrient assessment \\
Video analysis & OpenCV + MediaPipe + BioMistral & Every 30th frame as landmark coordinates & Exercise identification, then form assessment \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table*}

Llama3.1 constitutes the first stage of the chat pipeline. Before any response is generated, the model classifies the intent behind the user's query as exercise-related, injury-related, nutrition-related, making plans for either (including a separate category for plans with injuries), or general conversation.

Depending on the intent identified, the model takes the user's query, along with the retrieved context, and produces an initial response under a strict system prompt. Where the user's query is not the first message in the session, Llama3.1 condenses the preceding four queries into one and generates its response from this condensed form, which improves model comprehension and context retention. Where the intent is classified as an injury, the model additionally identifies the affected muscle and performs retrieval by querying the database for exercises that do not involve that muscle. If the intent is general conversation, no retrieval is performed and Llama3.1 responds in the register of a helpful assistant.

Qwen2.5 performs the role of a medical reviewer. Empirical evidence for this selection is presented in Section~\ref{sec:challenges}. It takes the response produced by Llama3.1, together with the retrieved context, and is supplied with either an exercise or nutrition review prompt according to the intent established. Both prompts specify three categories---`issues', `verdict' and `corrected response'. Issues are in turn divided into four categories: unsupported item, denoting the recommendation of an exercise absent from the retrieved context; wrong muscle targeting, denoting a claim that contradicts the information held in the database; factual error, such as stating that one exercise is easier than another where this contradicts the database; and safety, denoting missing warnings or unsafe instructions. The nutrition prompt includes only the factual error and safety categories. The model is instructed to flag only clear and concrete issues, and not to populate every category unnecessarily; vague wording and the omission of specific machine names are not treated as flaggable issues. It then returns a verdict of `safe', `needs correction' or `dangerous'. Where the verdict is `safe', the initial response is left unaltered; otherwise, a `corrected response' is generated. The entire output is structured and formatted as JSON, with each category represented as an object. Qwen2.5 is assigned this stage on account of its instruction-following capability. The corrected response, or the initial response where no correction is required, is then stored as a verified advice variable.

The final stage is handled by Llama3.1, acting as a rewriter. It retrieves the verified advice variable and rewrites it in markup, in an accessible and conversational register, substituting lay synonyms for medical terminology. Tokens are yielded individually and returned through a FastAPI streaming response endpoint \citep{fastapi}, which the frontend consumes and renders incrementally. As control passes between models in the pipeline, control tokens are yielded to indicate to the user that processing is ongoing, since response times across the pipeline average approximately 11 seconds.

Where the intent falls within plan-making, Llama3.1 invokes a structured chat function to format its response as a JSON representation of the plan. The JSON objects comprise the exercises retrieved through RAG (for plan-making, the retrieval limit is based on the number of days, with a minimum of 15 and a maximum of 28), together with days, sets, and repetitions. Where further clarification is required owing to underspecified user input, the model asks a single clarifying question that prompts the user for the missing information. Where the user still does not provide all information, the model continues asking until all required fields are populated. The schema constraining this output is given in Listing~\ref{lst:planschema}.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python,caption={The plan schema passed to Ollama's constrained decoding. Enumerations restrict the model to exercises actually returned by retrieval, preventing the recommendation of unsupported items.},label={lst:planschema},float,floatplacement=t]
def plan_schema(allowed_names, allowed_days):
    return {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "exercises": {"type": "array", "items": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "name": {"type": "string",
                     "enum": allowed_names},
            "day":  {"type": "string",
                     "enum": allowed_days},
            "sets": {"type": "integer",
                     "enum": [2, 3, 4, 5]},
            "reps": {"type": "integer",
                     "enum": [3, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 15]},
          },
          "required": ["name","day","sets","reps"]
        }},
      },
      "required": ["exercises"]
    }
\end{lstlisting}

The user's response is then formatted as JSON and used to complete the plan structure. The final plan JSON is retrieved by the frontend and rendered in markup in the chat, with a full weekly table view displayed in a `Plans' tab, and an optional monthly view. Altering plan details, such as number of sets, repetitions, exercises or days of the week, is done through the chat, with a manual option afforded in the `Plans' tab.
% TODO(draft): "EXPAND ON THIS" marker from the .docx was here.

Users may upload photographs and video for form analysis, physique assessment or food recommendation.

Qwen2.5-VL handles image analysis, taking the image uploaded by the user and analysing it in relation to the accompanying query. Neither retrieval nor review is applied at this stage, as the model performs adequately without them.

Video analysis is more involved. Once a video has been uploaded, every frame is passed through the OpenCV Python module \citep{opencv}, and every 30th frame is captured. Each captured frame is analysed by Google's MediaPipe module, which determines the $x$ and $y$ coordinates of all relevant body parts together with a visibility rating \citep{mediapipe}. The data for each frame are stored in a Python dictionary and then appended to a list. This list is then passed to BioMistral, which uses its contents together with the user query to describe the exercise being performed. On the basis of this description and its subsequent encoding, an SQL query is performed. The three closest results are presented to the user in the frontend as selectable buttons; where none is appropriate, the user can type a custom response. Following user confirmation, an SQL query retrieves the exercise description, which is passed to BioMistral together with the user's query and the list of frame coordinates in order to assess form.

Qwen2.5-VL is capable of bypassing the coordinate extraction and analysis stage by analysing the video directly, but doing so admits the possibility of intrinsic hallucination; the frame-extraction approach is therefore the more robust of the two. Requiring the model to confirm its interpretation with the user further improves the reliability of the final answer and reduces the likelihood of intrinsic hallucination.

\subsection{Fine-tuning}
\label{sec:finetuning}
The most frequent source of misinformation encountered by \citet{zaleski2024}, where the model asserts medical approval should be obtained before undertaking a form of exercise, was also observed repeatedly in the pipeline's test outputs. To reduce the frequency of this miscalibration, Llama3.1 was fine-tuned using QLoRA \citep{dettmers2023}. Only Llama3.1 was fine-tuned, as it serves as both first responder and final rewriter, so consistency of behaviour across the two roles is essential.

The scope of the fine-tune was narrowed deliberately during development. An initial plan to train on approximately 2,000 pairs covering output formatting and tool-calling behaviour was abandoned once each of those requirements proved addressable by other means: JSON structure through Ollama's constrained decoding, clarifying questions through null checking of an intake schema, exercise-specific facts through retrieval, and medical terminology simplification through a glossary supplied in the system prompt. Fine-tuning was therefore reserved for the disclaimer calibration described above, the one behaviour that prompting could not reliably produce.

Calibrating disclaimers requires a specification of what the appropriate disclaimer should be, and none had been defined. The base pipeline already produced safety disclaimers, but their placement and strength resulted from the model's own training rather than from deliberate design. A four-tier disclaimer policy was therefore written and implemented, ranging from Tier~0, a direct answer carrying no caveat, through Tier~1, a light caveat, and Tier~2, substantive deferral to a clinician, to Tier~3, refusal accompanied by direct referral. This policy constitutes the operational definition of `safe' against which the application is evaluated.

Training data took the form of preference pairs for Direct Preference Optimisation (DPO) \citep{rafailov2023}. The rejected response in each pair was generated by the local Llama3.1 model under a bare prompt; the chosen response was a minimal edit of that output, brought into conformity with the policy. Composing chosen responses independently was trialled and rejected, as it produced a three- to four-fold difference in length between the members of each pair, which would have trained the model on length and style rather than on calibration. Editing the existing response rather than replacing it kept the two roughly the same length. The editing role was delegated to DeepSeek-V3 \citep{deepseek2024}, selected in part to maintain complete separation of model families across the pipeline, discussed further in Section~\ref{sec:evaluation}.

A 50-sample validation run applied before full generation revealed that the editor was adding caution to Tier~0 items, inverting the intended relationship; the cause was that the editor inferred the target tier from the text and defaulted conservatively. Explicitly supplying the target tier corrected the direction, and interleaving tiers during generation ensured that a truncated run would not exhaust one tier before another. Generation produced 602 pairs, of which 582 remained after cleaning. The realised tier distribution was 146, 114, 224 and 98 for Tiers~0 to~3 respectively, skewed toward Tier~2 relative to an intended 35/30/25/10 split. This imbalance is relevant to the results reported below and is retained here as the actual rather than the target distribution.

Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct was loaded at 4-bit NF4 precision with double quantisation and an fp16 compute dtype. LoRA adapters were applied to the attention projections (\texttt{q\_proj}, \texttt{k\_proj}, \texttt{v\_proj}, \texttt{o\_proj}) with rank 16, alpha 32, dropout 0.05 and no trained bias terms. DPO used a beta of 0.1, a learning rate of $5\times10^{-5}$ on a cosine schedule with five warmup steps, and a per-device batch size of one with gradient accumulation over eight steps. As LoRA allows the reference policy to be recovered by disabling the adapter, no separate reference model was required.

Training was performed on a Google Colab T4 instance. Mixed-precision training was not viable on the T4 owing to a conflict between fp16 gradient scaling and the DPO trainer, so training proceeded in fp32; the resulting memory pressure required a maximum sequence length of 768 tokens, and in-loop evaluation to be disabled. A 10\% held-out split was retained for post hoc verification instead.

Prompts were templated in the chat format used by the application and deliberately excluded the disclaimer policy, so that the adapter was required to encode the policy rather than to rely on its presence in the context. Training loss fell from 0.68 at step 5 to 0.02 by step 20 and thereafter fluctuated without further improvement, indicating the model had begun memorising the small dataset; an initial longer run confirmed that no gains followed, and the final run was accordingly limited to 25 steps, reproducing the same trajectory. A checkpoint sweep indicated that Tier-1 over-escalation and Tier-3 escalation strength emerged together between steps 15 and 20, consistent with a single learned association between symptom cues and escalation, and attributable to the Tier-2-weighted dataset. Checkpoint 20 was selected over both 15 and 25: over 25 on tier-calibration behaviour rather than loss, which continued to fall while calibration did not improve; and over 15 because the coupling means Tier-3 escalation strength cannot be obtained without accepting some Tier-1 over-escalation.

A distinction should be drawn between the two forms of over-caution at issue here. The behaviour targeted by the tune is unnecessary deferral on unambiguous, low-risk queries. The residual errors after tuning are of a different kind: they cluster at the Tier~1 boundary, where the query is genuinely ambiguous, and they are uniformly in the conservative direction, in that the model does not reassure a symptomatic user. Over-caution on ambiguous queries is the appropriate failure mode for this domain; over-caution on unambiguous ones was the problem being solved. The extent to which the tune achieves this separation is reported in Section~\ref{sec:results}.

The trained adapter was converted to GGUF format via llama.cpp and loaded through Ollama's ADAPTER directive, avoiding a full fp16 merge of the base model. The model is served at Q4\_K\_M. This is not solely a storage decision: as reported in Section~\ref{sec:results}, the quantisation level itself affects calibration, with Q4\_K\_M attenuating the Tier-1 over-escalation present in the fp16 checkpoint while softening Tier-3 escalation. The resulting fine-tuned model replaces Llama3.1 in the answerer and rewriter stages of the pipeline. Tool calling and agentic functions are left to the base model, to avoid any degradation of instruction-following introduced by the tune. A comparison between the base and fine-tuned Llama3.1 is reported in Section~\ref{sec:results}.

\subsection{Challenges}
\label{sec:challenges}
Ensuring that small-parameter models adhere to a system prompt proved difficult, and several models were trialled before the final selection was settled. The initial rewriter model was Mistral \citep{jiang2023}, which greeted the user in every response despite explicit instruction to the contrary, and additionally leaked system information, for instance by stating that the output was a rewrite before presenting it.

Other medical LLMs, including Meditron:7B \citep{chen2023} and OpenMed \citep{openmed} were also evaluated, but the language of their responses was unnatural. OpenMed consistently rendered each query in the form of a forum thread, complete with users, usernames and comments, and disregarded the system prompt in the majority of cases.

BioMistral was intended to occupy a larger role within ReFit, but the model exhibited limitations incompatible with the intended pipeline. BioMistral is trained to answer short plaintext medical queries \citep{labrak2024} rather than to follow instructions. The first responder must process a verbose system prompt before generating output, which BioMistral handled adequately at first; as the prompt grew more complex, however, the model began to make significant formatting errors. Following retrieval, the response terminated after the colon on every occasion on which the model was about to recommend an exercise in list form. Attempts to engineer prompts that avoided list outputs were unsuccessful, and the model was therefore replaced as first responder by Llama3.1, which handles lists reliably.

The reviewer stage, also initially handled by BioMistral, originally applied a strict five-step criterion against which the initial response was graded; the model frequently terminated after the first step, labelling most advice as safe and contributing little of substance. Llama3.1 adhered more closely to the criteria in this role, but employing the same model to review its own outputs carries the difficulty that blind spots inherent to the model persist regardless of prompt engineering, allowing questionable advice to pass unflagged. Qwen2.5 was therefore adopted at this stage. BioMistral instead handles video analysis, where the expected output presents no formatting risk for the model.

Llava1.5 \citep{liu2024llava} was the initial image analysis model; the outputs of its 7B variant frequently misinterpreted both the image and the user query. Use of the 13B variant improved accuracy, but Qwen2.5-VL is more recent and performs better at recognising text within images, allowing macronutrient information to be extracted from a photograph alone.

\subsection{Evaluation}
\label{sec:evaluation}
The chat function is presented with a set of 50 predetermined questions, together with user medical and injury history as context. Care is taken to include vulnerable personas among the test users, as both \citet{zaleski2024} and \citet{lai2025} found LLMs to give less safe advice to elderly, chronically ill and disabled patients. The application's handling of such cases is central to determining whether LLMs can be considered `safe'. Responses are compared across an incrementally expanded set of configurations: base model, agentic pipeline, RAG, RAG with system prompts, fine-tuned model, and finally all preceding components combined. Only these configurations vary; the model, temperature, context length and question set remain constant.

\citet{zheng2023} report that LLM-as-judge systems compare favourably with human judgement, achieving an agreement rate of approximately 80\%. Accordingly, as a first step in the evaluation process, the resulting 300 outputs are judged by a cloud-based LLM (Claude Sonnet 5). This model forms no part of ReFit's architecture and is used solely offline within the evaluation process, preserving the privacy-focused design of the application.

The outputs are evaluated against the following criteria:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Factuality---correctness with respect to the provided information.
\item Faithfulness---adherence to the retrieved context, measured using the RAGAS Python framework \citep{es2025}.
\item Comprehensiveness---measured against a standard checklist rubric derived from \citet{pag2020}, and scored per category as present, partial or absent, following the methodology of \citet{zaleski2024}. For exercise responses the rubric records condition, safety screening, frequency, intensity, time, volume, progression, movements to avoid and grounding in the retrieved context; for nutrition it records relevant macronutrient breakdown, alignment with the Eatwell guidance, portion guidance, and caveats or limitations.
\item Safety---evidence of misleading advice or of omissions, flagged by severity (safe, mild, moderate, serious).
\item Tailoring---the degree to which the responses are adapted to specific users.
\item Readability---measured using Flesch-Kincaid tests, replicating \citet{zaleski2024}.
\end{enumerate}

Judgements are repeated 2--3 times to establish stability, with the order randomised on each occasion to mitigate position bias, whereby an LLM may favour certain positions in a list over others \citep{zheng2023}. The judge model belongs to a different family from both the local LLMs and the model used to synthesise the fine-tuning data. This mitigates self-enhancement bias, whereby LLM judges may assign inflated scores to their own generations \citep{zheng2023}.

The outputs are additionally assessed by human evaluators working in the medical field. Each evaluator is presented with 10 example questions, together with the accompanying user context, and asked to choose between two responses to the same question: one produced by the base model, Llama3.1, and the other by the full ReFit pipeline with RAG, reviewing and fine-tuning. The responses are presented as Response~A and Response~B without identifying the system that produced them. Evaluators then respond to four items, each with its own answer set:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Which response contains more dangerous advice, if either: Response~A, Response~B, neither, both equally concerning, or not sure.
\item Which response is the more factually accurate: Response~A, Response~B, both are accurate, or both contain errors.
\item Which response provides the more appropriate referral to a medical professional: Response~A, Response~B, both are appropriate, or both are inappropriate.
\item Which response the evaluator would trust more for the user described: Response~A, Response~B, or no preference.
\end{enumerate}

Evaluators are additionally invited to explain the reasoning behind their selections. The extent of agreement between the LLM judge and the human evaluators is then measured.

For the application to be considered `safe', its responses must meet a defined threshold: no flags of `serious' severity and fewer than 20\% flagged as `moderate' across the outputs of the full pipeline.

